<h1 class="doctitle">BARCODE Volumetric Metrics</h1>
<p class="subtitle">Formal specification of the metrics computed by <span class="mono">analysis/volumetric/</span></p>

<div class="front">

<p>This document is the formal counterpart to the published two-dimensional reference,
<i>BARCODE 2.0 Metrics</i>, and follows its conventions: numbered branches, one entry per
metric, numbered display equations, and a statement of units and failure conditions for every
quantity. Equation numbering here is section-scoped rather than continuous, so that inserting
an equation does not renumber the document.</p>

<p>The 2D reference defines <b>25</b> parameters across three branches. The volumetric
pipeline emits up to <b>64</b> across ten families. All 25 reappear — 17 under identical
names, 8 renamed <i>Area</i> to <i>Volume</i> — and the remaining 39 have no 2D counterpart.
Where a restated metric is <i>not</i> numerically comparable with its 2D namesake, this is
stated explicitly in the entry.</p>

<div class="note" style="border-left-color:#1a6b2a;background:#f2faf4">
<b style="color:#1a6b2a;font-variant:small-caps;letter-spacing:.03em">New capability:
BARCODE can now be given a segmentation</b>
<p style="margin-top:.35em">Published BARCODE has no notion of a segmentation — it decides
what counts as material by thresholding intensity at
$\overline{B}\times(1+\text{offset})$, and every structural metric inherits whatever that
threshold happens to catch. <b>The volumetric pipeline accepts an external segmentation mask,
and when one resolves it <i>replaces</i> the threshold entirely.</b> Objects become whatever
the segmenter says they are, rather than whatever survived a global intensity cut. This is the
largest behavioural difference from the 2D reference.</p>
<p>Four metric families cannot exist without one: <b>Surface Mesh</b> (§4.1–4.10), <b>Curvature</b>
(§4.11–4.16), <b>In-Mask Intensity</b> (§4.19), and <b>Packing Topology</b> (§4.20) — the last additionally
requiring integer instance labels. It also changes families that do exist without one: the
binarization branch (§1) then measures the segmented object, and flow and the intensity
histogram may be restricted to in-mask voxels
(<span class="mono">flow_use_mask</span>, on by default;
<span class="mono">intensity_use_mask</span>, off).</p>
<p style="margin-bottom:0">Formats: <span class="mono">.tif</span>/<span class="mono">.tiff</span>,
<span class="mono">.png</span>/<span class="mono">.bmp</span>, <span class="mono">.npy</span>,
<span class="mono">.npz</span> — including Cellpose <span class="mono">_seg.npy</span>
bundles. <b>Instance labels are preserved</b> rather than collapsed to a boolean, which is
what makes a confluent field measurable at all. Pairing is by regex and path template, so a
batch resolves automatically, and every validation <b>raises</b> rather than degrading (§P.3).</p>
</div>



