<h2 style="page-break-before:auto">§0 &nbsp; Notation and common reductions</h2>

<p>The analysed unit is a volume. Let $B(x,y,z,i)$ be the intensity of the voxel at position
$(x,y,z)$ in the $i^{th}$ timepoint, with $0\le x&lt;n_x$, $0\le y&lt;n_y$, $0\le z&lt;n_z$, so that
the analysed field contains</p>

$$\Nv \;=\; n_x\,n_y\,n_z \tag{0.1}$$

<p>voxels. Physical voxel dimensions are $(\Delta z,\Delta y,\Delta x)$ in micrometres, giving
a voxel volume</p>

$$v_0 \;=\; \Delta x\,\Delta y\,\Delta z \qquad [\mu\mathrm{m}^3] $$

<p>All of $\Nv$, $v_0$ and the axis extents refer to the <i>analysed</i> grid — that is, after
any isotropic resampling and cropping (§P.4), not the acquired grid.</p>

<h3>0.1 &nbsp; Timepoint selection</h3>

<p>Not every timepoint is analysed. Given $N_T$ timepoints and a stride $k$
(<span class="mono">frame_step</span>, default $10$), the analysed index set is</p>

$$\mathcal{F} \;=\; \{\,0,\,k,\,2k,\,\dots\,\}\cup\{N_T-1\},\qquad \Nf=|\mathcal{F}| \tag{0.2}$$

<p>where $k$ is first reduced by $k\leftarrow\max(\lfloor k/5\rfloor,1)$ while $k\ge N_T$, and
$\mathcal{F}=\{0\}$ when $N_T\le1$. The final index is always included. The optical flow branch
does not use $\mathcal{F}$ in this way; see §3.2.</p>

<h3>0.2 &nbsp; The three reductions</h3>

<p>Every metric produces one value per analysed timepoint and is then reduced by one of three
operators. Writing $\{q_i\}_{i\in\mathcal F}$ for the per-timepoint values:</p>

<p><b>Finite-only mean.</b> The default reduction, used unless stated otherwise:</p>

$$\big\langle q\big\rangle \;=\;
\frac{1}{|\mathcal{G}|}\sum_{i\in\mathcal{G}} q_i,
\qquad \mathcal{G}=\{\,i\in\mathcal F : q_i \text{ finite}\,\} \tag{0.3}$$

<p>with $\langle q\rangle = \mathrm{NaN}$ when $\mathcal G=\varnothing$. A timepoint that
failed therefore leaves the average rather than poisoning it, which also means a metric may be
finite while most timepoints failed.</p>

<p><b>Top-decile mean.</b> Used by the four <i>maximum</i> metrics. Let $q_{(1)}\ge q_{(2)}\ge
\dots$ be the values in decreasing order and $n_{10}=\lceil 0.1\,\Nf\rceil$:</p>

$$\mathrm{top}_{10}(q) \;=\; \frac{1}{n_{10}}\sum_{j=1}^{n_{10}} q_{(j)} \tag{0.4}$$

<p class="eqnote">The decile fraction $0.1$ is fixed in the source and is not a user parameter.</p>

<p><b>Head–tail comparison.</b> Used by every <i>change</i> metric. With
$f$ = <span class="mono">percentage_frames_evaluated</span> (default $0.05$) and</p>

$$n_e=\max\big(\lceil f\,\Nf\rceil,\,1\big) \tag{0.5}$$

<p>the head and tail means are $\mu_{\text{head}}=\langle q_{i}\rangle_{i\in\text{first }n_e}$
and $\mu_{\text{tail}}=\langle q_{i}\rangle_{i\in\text{last }n_e}$. The binarization branch
forms a <i>ratio</i> and the intensity and flow branches a <i>difference</i>:</p>

$$\Delta^{\text{ratio}} q=\frac{\mu_{\text{tail}}}{\mu_{\text{head}}},
\qquad
\Delta^{\text{diff}} q=\mu_{\text{tail}}-\mu_{\text{head}} \tag{0.6}$$

<div class="note"><b>Single-timepoint rule.</b> When $\Nf&lt;2$ every change metric returns
$\mathrm{NaN}$, not $0$ or $1$. A single volume is the ordinary case in this pipeline, so this
is encountered constantly. The 2D branch instead compares a frame with itself and returns
$0$ (differences) or $1$ (ratios).</div>

<h3>0.3 &nbsp; Size reporting: fraction and quantity</h3>

<p>Every size metric is reported twice. For a raw voxel count $s$,</p>

$$s^{\,\text{frac}}=\frac{s}{\Nv}\quad[\,\text{dimensionless, }0\!-\!1\,],
\qquad
s^{\,\mathcal Q}=s\,v_0\quad[\mu\mathrm{m}^3] \tag{0.7}$$

<p>The fractional column carries the unit label <span class="mono">fraction of FOV</span> but holds a
fraction in $[0,1]$, following the 2D convention. The two forms are <i>not</i>
interchangeable: $s^{\text{frac}}$ depends on the crop (§P.4) and $s^{\mathcal Q}$ does not, so
only $s^{\mathcal Q}$ is comparable between runs of differing crop.</p>

<div class="warn"><span class="h">The quantity columns cannot currently be written</span>
See defect D5 (§6): the CSV writer's physical-units path asserts a 2D-only invariant and
raises on any 3D run with more than one row.</div>

<h2>§P &nbsp; Measurement preconditions</h2>

<p>Every quantity in this document is conditioned on the following. A reader who does not know
which of these applied cannot interpret any reported number.</p>

<h3>P.1 &nbsp; Axes are declared, never inferred</h3>
<p>The reader takes the file's declared axis order and raises on anything it cannot interpret:
the placeholder axes <span class="mono">I</span>, <span class="mono">Q</span>,
<span class="mono">S</span>; any axis outside <span class="mono">TZCYX</span>; a missing
$Y$ or $X$; and a missing $Z$. It never falls back on shape heuristics. An
<span class="mono">axes_override</span> may <i>tell</i> it the true order — one letter per data
dimension — which replaces the declared order outright.</p>

<h3>P.2 &nbsp; Voxel size and frame interval</h3>
<p>Precedence is explicit setting $\to$ file metadata $\to$ warn and default to
$1.0\,\mu\mathrm{m}$. The $z$ step falls back to the ImageJ <span class="mono">spacing</span>
tag and the $xy$ step to $\mu\mathrm{m}_{\text{per unit}}/\mathrm{px}_{\text{per unit}}$ from
the resolution tag.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Resolution tags are read through a unit name, not assumed to be
microns.</b> The unit comes from ImageJ's <span class="mono">unit</span> field, else the TIFF
<span class="mono">ResolutionUnit</span>. A tag naming an unrecognised unit, or one declaring
the image explicitly uncalibrated (<span class="mono">pixel</span>,
<span class="mono">px</span>, <span class="mono">none</span>,
<span class="mono">a.u.</span>), is discarded — it warns and falls back to
$1.0\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ rather than reading a pixel count as microns. Only a wholly absent unit
takes the older "assume microns" path.</div>
<div class="note"><b>The frame interval must be set by hand.</b> It defaults to $0$, meaning
"fall back to ImageJ <span class="mono">finterval</span>, then to $1.0$". On stacks exported
one timepoint per file that tag frequently describes the $z$ acquisition, producing a number
that looks like seconds but is per-frame. Since $v\propto 1/\Delta t$ exactly and nothing else
changes, the error is invisible in the output.</div>

<h3>P.3 &nbsp; Segmentation replaces thresholding</h3>
<p><b>New in the volumetric pipeline; published BARCODE has no segmentation input.</b> Set
<span class="mono">segmentation_enabled</span> and point the resolver at the masks. Supported
formats are <span class="mono">.tif</span>/<span class="mono">.tiff</span>,
<span class="mono">.png</span>/<span class="mono">.bmp</span>, <span class="mono">.npy</span>
and <span class="mono">.npz</span>, dispatched on the suffix, where a
<span class="mono">.npy</span> may be a bare label array or a Cellpose
<span class="mono">_seg.npy</span> bundle unwrapped through its
<span class="mono">masks</span>/<span class="mono">mask</span>/<span class="mono">labels</span>
key. A second independent mask may accompany the first, so a nucleus and its cell are both
available for one image.</p>
<p>When a mask resolves it <i>is</i> the binarization: the threshold, minimum island size and
inversion are bypassed for the structural branch, while intensity and autocorrelation continue
to use raw voxels. Validation raises rather than degrading — $XY$ dimensions must match
exactly, and the $z$ extents must agree to within $1.0\,\mu\mathrm{m}$:</p>

$$\big|\,n_z\,\Delta z \;-\; n_z^{\text{mask}}\,\Delta z^{\text{mask}}\big| \;\le\; 1.0\ \mu\mathrm{m} \tag{P.1}$$

<p>A mask carrying more than one distinct positive value keeps its integer labels; collapsing
it to a boolean would re-derive objects by connectivity and merge every touching instance.</p>

<h3>P.4 &nbsp; Isotropic resampling and the crop box</h3>
<div class="note"><b>None of this occurs without a segmentation.</b> With no mask the volumes
are analysed on the acquired anisotropic grid, uncropped, whatever
<span class="mono">make_isotropic</span> is set to.</div>
<p>With a mask and <span class="mono">make_isotropic</span> enabled: the image is resampled
onto the mask grid (linear interpolation), then both onto an isotropic grid at the $xy$
spacing — images linearly, masks by nearest neighbour, since any interpolating kernel would
average neighbouring instance labels into new and meaningless ones. Where the mask grid
overhangs the image the last acquired plane is repeated rather than zero-filled; writing
black voxels there fed the mean-relative threshold, the histogram and every fraction
denominator while the mask still declared foreground.</p>
<p>It does <b>not</b> crop. <span class="mono">crop_to_mask</span> defaults to
<b>False</b>, so $\Nv$ in (0.1) is the acquired field of view.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Why cropping is off.</b> Every fractional size in (0.7) is a fraction
<i>of the analysed box</i>. Cropping to the mask gives each file its own denominator, so the
same object in a tighter box reports a larger fraction and a genuine size trend cannot be
told apart from the box tracking the object. Turning it on restores the old behaviour and
crops a series to the <i>union</i> of the per-frame boxes, never each to its own. The
$\mu\mathrm{m}^3$ quantity columns are unaffected either way — prefer them when in doubt.
The intended way to analyse less than the full field is an explicit $z$ range.</div>

<h3>P.5 &nbsp; Range restriction</h3>
<p>The analysed ranges satisfy $z\in[z_0,z_1]$ and $i\in[t_0,t_1]$, <b>inclusive of both
ends</b>, with $\text{end}=0$ meaning "to the end" and negative values counting back from the
end ($-1$ being the last). So $z_0=12,\;z_1=46$ analyses slices 12 through <b>46</b> — 35
slices — and $z_0=z_1$ is a single slice rather than an empty range. An empty
or reversed range raises. Any restriction sets flag digit 5. Because "slice 46" is ambiguous
between the acquired and isotropic grids (54 versus $\approx249$ planes on
$0.3/0.065\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ data), the $z$ range carries an explicit unit: acquired index,
isotropic index, or micrometres of depth. In <span class="mono">xyzt</span> the acquired range
is mapped onto a finer mask grid by $\mathrm{round}\!\big(i\,(m_z-1)/(n_z-1)\big)$, so a
restricted image and its full-depth mask stay aligned over the sub-range rather than the mask
being planted at $z=0$.</p>
<p><span class="mono">t_range_units="seconds"</span> converts through the <b>configured</b>
frame interval, falling back to the file's tag only when the file stated timing and otherwise
raising. Before that, a guard that could never fire meant "the first 600 seconds" selected 600
timepoints on a 60-second series.</p>

<h2 class="branch">1 &nbsp; Image Binarization (3D) Branch</h2>

<p>The structural branch. Twelve columns, measuring volumes with 26-connectivity where the 2D
branch measures areas with 8-connectivity. Throughout this section a <i>frame</i> means one
timepoint — an entire $Z$-stack — not a $z$-slice.</p>

<h3>1.0 &nbsp; The binary volume</h3>

<p>Absent a segmentation, the threshold is the volume mean scaled by a user offset (default
$0.1$), computed over all voxels of the $i^{th}$ volume:</p>

$$\overline{B}(i) \;=\; \frac{1}{\Nv}\sum_{x,y,z} B(x,y,z,i) \tag{1.1}$$

$$b(x,y,z,i)=\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if } B(x,y,z,i)\;\ge\;\overline{B}(i)\times(1+\text{offset})\\[2pt]
0, & \text{if } B(x,y,z,i)\;<\;\overline{B}(i)\times(1+\text{offset})
\end{cases} \tag{1.2}$$

<p>Connected objects and holes smaller than $s_{\min}+1$ voxels are then removed under
26-connectivity, where $s_{\min}$ is <span class="mono">minimum_island_size</span> (default
$1$, so isolated single voxels are discarded). If inversion is selected, $b\leftarrow 1-b$.</p>

<div class="note"><b>A constant volume short-circuits to an empty binary.</b> A blank or
flat-saturated acquisition has zero range, and (1.1)–(1.2) would then give a threshold of $0$
and mark <i>every</i> voxel as foreground — one island filling the field, $C=1$, and a CSV row
indistinguishable from a genuinely percolating sample.</div>

<p class="eqnote">There is no spatial binning in 3D: the 2D branch's $p_b$ has no analogue
here, and (0.1) is the unbinned voxel count.</p>

<p>Let $\{\mathcal{R}_1(i),\dots,\mathcal{R}_{n_i}(i)\}$ be the 26-connected foreground
components of frame $i$, ordered so that $|\mathcal{R}_1|\ge|\mathcal{R}_2|\ge\dots$, where
$|\cdot|$ denotes voxel count. When an instance mask supplies labels, that partition is used
directly instead of connected-component labelling.</p>

<h3>1.1 &nbsp; Connectivity $C$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Connectivity</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> fraction of frames, valued $0\!-\!1$</div>
<p>The fraction of analysed frames in which a single component percolates the volume. Labelling
under the full $3\times3\times3$ structuring element, let $\mathcal{L}^{a}_{0}$ and
$\mathcal{L}^{a}_{1}$ be the sets of non-zero labels appearing on the two opposing faces normal
to axis $a$. Then</p>

$$C_i=\begin{cases}
1, & \text{if } \exists\,a\in\{x,y,z\}:\ \mathcal{L}^{a}_{0}\cap\mathcal{L}^{a}_{1}\neq\varnothing\\[2pt]
0, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\qquad
C=\frac{1}{\Nf}\sum_{i\in\mathcal F} C_i \tag{1.3}$$

<p><b>Versus 2D.</b> The 2D metric tests two axes under 8-connectivity; this tests three under
26-connectivity, so an object spanning depth but not the lateral field now scores $1$. Never
$\mathrm{NaN}$.</p>

<h3>1.2 &nbsp; Maximum island volume $I$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Maximum Island Volume</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> fraction of FOV ($0\!-\!1$); quantity form $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$</div>
<p>The size of the largest component, reduced by the top-decile mean (0.4) and
normalised by (0.7):</p>

$$I \;=\; \frac{\mathrm{top}_{10}\big(|\mathcal{R}_1(i)|\big)}{\Nv},
\qquad
I^{\mathcal Q} \;=\; \mathrm{top}_{10}\big(|\mathcal{R}_1(i)|\big)\, v_0 \tag{1.4}$$

<p>$\mathrm{NaN}$ when every analysed frame is empty.</p>
<p><b>Versus 2D.</b> The 2D "Area Quantity" multiplies a pixel count by the micron-per-pixel
ratio <i>once</i>, yielding $\mu\mathrm{m}\!\cdot\!\mathrm{px}$ rather than
$\mu\mathrm{m}^2$ — dimensionally inconsistent. Equation (0.7) multiplies by the
full voxel volume and is a true $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$.</p>

<h3>1.3 &nbsp; Maximum void volume $V$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Maximum Void Volume</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> fraction of FOV ($0\!-\!1$); quantity form $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$</div>
<p>As (1.4) but over the 26-connected components of the complement $1-b$.</p>
<div class="warn"><span class="h">Edge case — a deliberate divergence from 2D</span>
If the foreground fills the volume so that no background component exists, the 3D branch
reports $0$ — the minimum, which is what "no void exists" means. The 2D branch returns the
whole frame instead, reporting the <i>maximum</i> and inverting the metric on exactly that
case, and it feeds $\Delta V$ as well, so the 2D and 3D columns are not comparable on a filled
field.</div>

<h3>1.4 &nbsp; Maximum island volume change $\Delta I$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Maximum Island Volume Change</span>
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> ratio to initial &nbsp;·&nbsp; no quantity form</div>
<p>The ratio form of (0.6), evaluated on raw voxel counts so that the result is
scale-free:</p>

$$\Delta I \;=\;
\frac{\big\langle |\mathcal{R}_1(i)| \big\rangle_{\text{last } n_e}}
     {\big\langle |\mathcal{R}_1(i)| \big\rangle_{\text{first } n_e}} \tag{1.5}$$

<p>$\mathrm{NaN}$ when $\Nf<2$. Values above $1$ indicate growth.</p>

<h3>1.5 &nbsp; Maximum void volume change $\Delta V$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Maximum Void Volume Change</span>
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> ratio to initial</div>
<p>Equation (1.5) applied to the void series, with the same single-frame rule.</p>

<h3>1.6 &nbsp; Initial maximum and 2nd maximum island volumes $I_{0,1},\,I_{0,2}$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Initial Maximum Island Volume</span>,
<span class="mono">Initial 2nd Maximum Island Volume</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> fraction of FOV ($0\!-\!1$); quantity forms $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$</div>

$$I_{0,1}=\frac{1}{\Nv}\big\langle |\mathcal{R}_1(i)| \big\rangle_{\text{first } n_e},
\qquad
I_{0,2}=\frac{1}{\Nv}\big\langle |\mathcal{R}_2(i)| \big\rangle_{\text{first } n_e} \tag{1.6}$$

<p>with the convention $|\mathcal{R}_2(i)|=0$ when frame $i$ contains a single object. Hence
$I_{0,2}=0$ — a meaningful statement, not a missing value — for a single-nucleus run. Unlike
the change metrics, these are <i>not</i> $\mathrm{NaN}$ for a single timepoint.</p>

<h3>1.7 &nbsp; Mean island anisotropy $A_I$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mean Island Anisotropy</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless, $\ge 1$</div>
<p>Let $\lambda_1\ge\lambda_2\ge\lambda_3$ be the eigenvalues of the inertia tensor of a
component. The equivalent-ellipsoid major and minor axis lengths are</p>

$$a=\sqrt{10\,\max(\lambda_1+\lambda_2-\lambda_3,\,0)},
\qquad
c=\sqrt{10\,\max(-\lambda_1+\lambda_2+\lambda_3,\,0)} \tag{1.7}$$

$$A^{k}_{i}=\begin{cases}
a/c, & c > 10^{-6}\\
\mathrm{NaN}, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\qquad
A_I=\Big\langle\ \big\langle A^{k}_{i}\big\rangle_{k}\ \Big\rangle_{i} \tag{1.8}$$

<div class="note"><b>Measured in physical coordinates, so it describes the object, not the
sampling grid.</b> The inertia tensor is built with the physical voxel spacing, so a physically
spherical object reads ≈1 on any grid — where an index-space tensor would report the grid's own
aspect ratio (e.g. 4.6 on the $z$ 0.3 / $xy$ 0.065 µm Jurkat geometry). On an isotropic grid the
distinction vanishes: uniform scaling multiplies every eigenvalue by $s^2$, leaving the ratio
unchanged.</div>
<div class="note">The clamp in (1.7) is not cosmetic. The library's axis-length
properties raise a domain error on flat or thin 3D regions; on one real nucleus <b>135 of 167</b>
components were degenerate. Degenerate components are excluded from the mean, not zeroed.
Consequently $A_I$ is a 3D ellipsoid ratio and is <i>not</i> comparable with the 2D ellipse
ratio.</div>

<h3>1.8 &nbsp; Mean island volume $\bar I$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mean Island Volume</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> fraction of FOV ($0\!-\!1$); quantity form $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$</div>

$$\bar I=\frac{1}{\Nv}\left\langle \frac{1}{n_i}\sum_{k=1}^{n_i}\big|\mathcal{R}_k(i)\big| \right\rangle_i \tag{1.9}$$

<h3>1.9 &nbsp; Total island volume $I_{tot}$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Total Island Volume</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> fraction of FOV ($0\!-\!1$); quantity form $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$</div>
<p>The foreground volume fraction of the analysed field:</p>

$$I_{tot}=\frac{1}{\Nv}\left\langle \sum_{k=1}^{n_i}\big|\mathcal{R}_k(i)\big| \right\rangle_i \tag{1.10}$$

<p>With a segmentation, $I_{tot}^{\mathcal Q}$ is effectively the segmented volume in
$\mu\mathrm{m}^3$ — the branch's most direct measure of object volume, and the natural
cross-check against the mesh volume of §4.1.</p>

<h3>1.10 &nbsp; Mean island separation $D_I$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mean Island Separation</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}$ &nbsp;·&nbsp; no quantity form</div>
<p>Component centroids are scaled into physical coordinates,
$\mathbf{c}_k=(z_k\Delta z,\,y_k\Delta y,\,x_k\Delta x)$, so the metric is natively in
micrometres. With $\gamma$ = <span class="mono">neighbor_island_fraction</span> (default
$0.1$) the neighbour count is</p>

$$\kappa=\min\!\big(\max(\lceil \gamma\, n_i\rceil-1,\,1),\ n_i-1\big) \tag{1.11}$$

<p>and, writing $\mathcal{N}_\kappa(k)$ for the $\kappa$ nearest centroids to $\mathbf{c}_k$
<i>excluding $\mathbf{c}_k$ itself</i>,</p>

$$D_I=\left\langle\ \frac{1}{n_i\,\kappa}\sum_{k=1}^{n_i}\ \sum_{l\in\mathcal{N}_\kappa(k)}
\big\|\mathbf{c}_k-\mathbf{c}_l\big\|\ \right\rangle_i \tag{1.12}$$

<p>$\mathrm{NaN}$ whenever a frame holds fewer than two components — the ordinary case for a
single segmented nucleus.</p>
<div class="warn"><span class="h">Not comparable with the 2D metric</span>
The 2D implementation takes the $\kappa+1$ smallest entries of each row of the distance matrix
<i>including the diagonal zero</i>, so its mean is deflated by exactly $\kappa/(\kappa+1)$.
Equation (1.12) excludes the self-distance. The 2D version also works in isotropic
pixel units and rescales at the end, where this scales each axis by its own spacing
beforehand.</div>

<h3>1.11 &nbsp; Structural correlation length $\xi_I$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Structural Correlation Length</span>
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}$</div>

<p>Computed on the <i>raw</i> voxels — never the binary and never the mask, even when a
segmentation replaced thresholding. Normalising to zero mean and unit variance,</p>

$$B'(x,y,z,i)=\frac{B(x,y,z,i)-\overline{B}(i)}{\sigma_B(i)} \tag{1.13}$$

<p>the autocorrelation follows from the Wiener–Khinchin theorem, using the full 3D transform
with no zero padding, so it is the <i>circular</i> autocorrelation:</p>

$$G(\mathbf{\Delta},i)=\frac{1}{\Nv}\,
\mathcal{F}^{-1}\Big[\big|\mathcal{F}\{B'\}\big|^{2}\Big](\mathbf{\Delta}),
\qquad G(\mathbf{0},i)\simeq 1 \tag{1.14}$$

<p>The 3D field is then radially averaged over shells of physical radius. With
$\delta=\min(\Delta x,\Delta y,\Delta z)$ and shell edges $r_j=j\delta$ bounded by</p>

$$r_{\max}=\min\!\Big(\tfrac{n_z}{2}\Delta z,\ \tfrac{n_y}{2}\Delta y,\ \tfrac{n_x}{2}\Delta x\Big) \tag{1.15}$$

<p>each shell $\mathcal{S}_j=\{\mathbf{\Delta}: r_j\le\|\mathbf{\Delta}\|&lt;r_{j+1}\}$
reports both its mean correlation and <i>its own mean radius</i>:</p>

$$g_j=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}_j|}\sum_{\mathbf{\Delta}\in \mathcal{S}_j} G(\mathbf{\Delta}),
\qquad
\rho_j=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{S}_j|}\sum_{\mathbf{\Delta}\in \mathcal{S}_j}\|\mathbf{\Delta}\| \tag{1.16}$$

<p class="eqnote">Three departures from the 2D branch, each deliberate: shells are binned on
<i>physical</i> distance so anisotropic voxels do not distort the profile; $r_{\max}$ is bounded
by the smallest physical half-extent across all three axes rather than a $y$-index assumption;
and each shell is anchored at $\rho_j$ rather than at the bin's left edge. A shell's population
grows as $r^2$, so its mean radius lies well above its left edge, and edge-anchoring makes the
profile appear to decay faster than it does. A consequence of $\rho_j$ is that reported lengths
are <i>not</i> integer multiples of the voxel size, unlike in 2D.</p>

<p>With threshold $\tau=e^{-1}$, let $j^\ast$ be the smallest $j$ with $g_j>\tau$ and
$g_{j+1}\le\tau$ — the first downward crossing. Then</p>

$$\xi_I(i)=\begin{cases}
\rho_{j^\ast}, & \big|g_{j^\ast}-\tau\big| \;<\; \big|g_{j^\ast+1}-\tau\big|\\[2pt]
\rho_{j^\ast+1}, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases}
\qquad
\xi_I=\big\langle \xi_I(i)\big\rangle \tag{1.17}$$

<div class="warn"><span class="h">This is not the reference-release definition</span>
Equation (1.17) snaps to whichever adjacent shell's <i>correlation value</i> lies
closer to $\tau$, and can therefore return a radius at which the correlation is still
<i>above</i> threshold. The BARCODE 2.0 release that produced the published reference CSVs took
the first radius at which $g\le\tau$ — always the outer point — so reported lengths here are
systematically <b>smaller</b>. This matches the current 2D branch verbatim, by design, so that
2D and 3D agree with each other rather than with the reference.</div>

<p>$\xi_I(i)=\mathrm{NaN}$ when the volume is constant, when $r_{\max}\le\delta$, or when no
downward crossing exists within $r_{\max}$ — the last being the "correlation length exceeds the
field of view" case that raises flag digit 3.</p>
<p class="eqnote">A subtlety: an empty shell yields $g_j=\mathrm{NaN}$, and NaN comparisons are
false, so a gap in the profile silently blocks a crossing at that index. This is a second route
to flag 3 beyond the documented one.</p>

<h2 class="branch">2 &nbsp; Intensity Distribution Branch</h2>

<p>Metrics 2.1–2.6 restate the published branch with a volume in place of a plane; since the
histogram flattens its input, the arithmetic on a given voxel set is identical. Metrics
2.7–2.10 are new: they are the branch's first <i>extensive</i> quantities, scaling with the
amount of material rather than describing the shape of the histogram.</p>

<h3>2.0 &nbsp; The histogram</h3>

<p>Per analysed timepoint the voxel values are binned into $N_b$ bins
(<span class="mono">bin_size</span>, default $300$) over the data's own range — there is no
fixed range and no background exclusion. Writing $\beta_j$ for the bin centres and $c_j$ for
the counts, the probabilities are $p_j=c_j/\sum_l c_l$. Bins with $p_j\le\eta$ are then
<i>discarded</i> ($\eta$ = <span class="mono">noise_threshold</span>, default $5\times10^{-4}$)
and the survivors renormalised:</p>

$$\mathcal{B}=\{\,j : p_j > \eta\,\},
\qquad
\hat p_j=\frac{p_j}{\sum_{l\in\mathcal{B}} p_l},\quad j\in\mathcal{B} \tag{2.1}$$

<p class="eqnote">This is a <i>bin-population</i> filter, not an intensity filter: it removes
sparsely populated bins wherever in the range they lie.</p>

<p>All moments are probability-weighted sums over the surviving centres:</p>

$$\mu_i=\sum_{j\in\mathcal{B}}\beta_j\hat p_j,
\qquad
\sigma_i=\Big(\sum_{j\in\mathcal{B}}(\beta_j-\mu_i)^2\hat p_j\Big)^{1/2} \tag{2.2}$$

$$\beta^{\text{mode}}_i=\beta_{\arg\max_j \hat p_j},
\qquad
\beta^{\text{med}}_i=\min\Big\{\beta_j : \textstyle\sum_{l\le j}\hat p_l\ge \tfrac12\Big\} \tag{2.3}$$

<div class="note"><b>Optional mask restriction.</b> With
<span class="mono">intensity_use_mask</span> (default off) the histogram is built only from
in-mask voxels; for an instance mask this means <i>any</i> non-zero label, so all objects pool.
This changes the quantity rather than refining it — on one test volume it moved the maximum
kurtosis from $9.93$ to $-0.11$ — so masked and unmasked runs must not be pooled.</div>

<h3>2.1 &nbsp; Maximum kurtosis $K$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Maximum Kurtosis</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless</div>

$$K_i=\frac{\sum_{j\in\mathcal{B}}(\beta_j-\mu_i)^4\,\hat p_j}{\sigma_i^{4}}-3,
\qquad
K=\mathrm{top}_{10}(K_i) \tag{2.4}$$

<p>The $-3$ makes a Gaussian read $0$. Division by $\sigma_i^4$ is unguarded, so a constant
volume yields $\pm\infty$ or $\mathrm{NaN}$.</p>

<h3>2.2 &nbsp; Maximum median skewness $S_1$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Maximum Median Skewness</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless</div>
<p>Pearson's second coefficient. Positive means the bulk lies below the mean — a dim body with
a bright tail.</p>

$$S_1(i)=\frac{3\big(\mu_i-\beta^{\text{med}}_i\big)}{\sigma_i},
\qquad
S_1=\mathrm{top}_{10}\big(S_1(i)\big) \tag{2.5}$$

<h3>2.3 &nbsp; Maximum mode skewness $S_2$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Maximum Mode Skewness</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless</div>
<p>Pearson's first coefficient, referenced to the histogram peak rather than the median, so it
responds to the background peak where $S_1$ responds to the body of the distribution.</p>

$$S_2(i)=\frac{\mu_i-\beta^{\text{mode}}_i}{\sigma_i},
\qquad
S_2=\mathrm{top}_{10}\big(S_2(i)\big) \tag{2.6}$$

<h3>2.4–2.6 &nbsp; Change metrics $\Delta K,\ \Delta S_1,\ \Delta S_2$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Kurtosis Change</span>,
<span class="mono">Median Skewness Change</span>, <span class="mono">Mode Skewness Change</span>
— suffixed <span class="mono">(over Z)</span> in <span class="mono">xyz</span> mode
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> dimensionless</div>
<p>The <i>difference</i> form of (0.6), in contrast to the binarization branch's ratio:</p>

$$\Delta K=\langle K_i\rangle_{\text{last }n_e}-\langle K_i\rangle_{\text{first }n_e},
\quad\text{and likewise for } S_1,\ S_2 \tag{2.7}$$

<p>$\mathrm{NaN}$ when $\Nf<2$. In <span class="mono">xyz</span> mode the progression axis is
<i>depth</i>, not time, and the columns are renamed accordingly — the rename is the safety
mechanism, since a depth trend read as a time trend is a silent scientific error.</p>

<h3>2.7 &nbsp; Total intensity $\Phi$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Total Intensity</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> a.u. &nbsp;·&nbsp; opt-in</div>
<p>Let $\mathcal{V}_i$ be the set of finite voxel values considered at timepoint $i$ (all of
them, or the in-mask subset).</p>

$$\Phi=\Big\langle \sum_{u\in\mathcal{V}_i} u \Big\rangle_i \tag{2.8}$$

<p>The branch's only genuinely extensive quantity: it doubles when the object doubles, which no
shape metric does. Two caveats — it is a raw sum <i>including background</i> unless the mask
restriction is on, and it is meaningless if the detector clipped, so flag digit 2 should be
checked first.</p>

<h3>2.8–2.9 &nbsp; Mean intensity $\bar\Phi$ and intensity SD $\sigma_\Phi$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Mean Intensity</span>,
<span class="mono">Intensity SD</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> a.u.</div>

$$\bar\Phi=\Big\langle \tfrac{1}{|\mathcal{V}_i|}\textstyle\sum_{u} u \Big\rangle_i,
\qquad
\sigma_\Phi=\Big\langle \big[\tfrac{1}{|\mathcal{V}_i|}\textstyle\sum_{u}(u-\bar u_i)^2\big]^{1/2} \Big\rangle_i \tag{2.9}$$

<p>The standard deviation is the <i>population</i> form ($\mathrm{ddof}=0$). Being per-sample,
$\bar\Phi$ is invariant to voxel size, unlike $\rho_\Phi$ below.</p>

<h3>2.10 &nbsp; Intensity density $\rho_\Phi$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Intensity Density (per volume)</span> in a
3D mode, <span class="mono">(per area)</span> in a 2D mode &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> a.u./$\mu\mathrm{m}^3$ or a.u./$\mu\mathrm{m}^2$</div>

$$\rho_\Phi=\Big\langle \frac{\sum_{u\in\mathcal{V}_i} u}{|\mathcal{V}_i|\;v_0} \Big\rangle_i \tag{2.10}$$

<p>Signal per unit physical volume on the <i>analysed</i> grid, so unlike $\bar\Phi$ it does
change with voxel size. $\mathrm{NaN}$ when the spacing is degenerate. In
<span class="mono">xyz</span> mode the sample size is the in-plane pixel area, the $z$ factor
being $1$.</p>

<h2 class="branch">3 &nbsp; Optical Flow (3D) Branch</h2>

<div class="note"><b>A static z-stack gets no flow columns at all.</b> When the branch produces nothing — a single timepoint, or a series shorter than the window — the seven flow columns are dropped from the CSV and the barcode rather than written as all-NaN black stripes. Volumetric modes only; the 2D schema is unchanged, and such a CSV reads back correctly.</div>
<div class="warn"><span class="h">Provisional — treat as work in progress</span>
The 3D flow branch is functional and wired in, but its <b>absolute values are not yet validated
for publication</b>. The solver systematically under-reports speed (§3.3) and assumes small
displacements (§3.1). Use it for <i>relative</i> comparisons within a consistent dataset, not
for an absolute µm/s. Three of the seven columns are also not comparable with their 2D
namesakes, and Speed only relatively.</div>

<p>Seven columns matching the published 3.1–3.7, computed by a different solver on a different
temporal unit.</p>

<h3>3.1 &nbsp; The solver</h3>

<p>Not Farnebäck — no three-dimensional equivalent exists in the library — but Lucas–Kanade
with Gaussian-derivative gradients and a Gaussian-weighted structure tensor. It is a
<b>differential</b> method and assumes <b>small displacements</b> between consecutive volumes;
there is no coarse-to-fine pyramid, so large motion is under-read. It fits BARCODE's philosophy
— CPU-only, no GPU, no training, runnable by any user — the same trade the 2D Farnebäck branch
makes. Each analysed timepoint requires a <i>contiguous</i> window of</p>

$$W=6\,\sigma_t+1 \tag{3.1}$$

<p>volumes centred on it, $W=7$ at the default $\sigma_t=1$. The temporal derivative is taken
across that window; <b>only the central volume is then retained</b>, and every spatial
derivative and the whole structure tensor are formed from it alone. Writing $\mathbf{v}$ for
the unknown velocity, $\nabla I$ for the spatial gradient and $I_t$ for the temporal
derivative, the weighted normal equations are</p>

$$\Big(\underbrace{\textstyle\sum_{\mathcal{W}} w\,\nabla I\,\nabla I^{\!\top}}_{\textstyle \mathsf{M}}\Big)\,\mathbf{v}
\;=\;-\sum_{\mathcal{W}} w\,\nabla I\,I_t \tag{3.2}$$

<p>where $w$ is an isotropic Gaussian of width $\sigma_w$ (default $4$ voxels) and
$\mathsf{M}$ is the $3\times3$ structure tensor. The system is solved in closed form with
machine epsilon added to $\det\mathsf{M}$ as a divide-by-zero guard. The <i>reliability</i> at
each voxel is the smallest eigenvalue,</p>

$$r=\lambda_{\min}(\mathsf{M}) \tag{3.3}$$

<p>Velocities emerge in voxels per frame and are converted to $\mu\mathrm{m}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$:</p>

$$v_x=\frac{u_x\,\Delta x}{\Delta t},\qquad
v_y=\frac{u_y\,\Delta y}{\Delta t},\qquad
v_z=\frac{u_z\,\Delta z}{\Delta t} \tag{3.4}$$

<div class="note"><b>Where the $y$-up convention lives.</b> BARCODE reports directions $y$-up
while the array indexes $y$ downward. The 2D branch reconciles this by reversing the array
axis <i>and</i> negating the component, leaving the field self-consistent. The 3D branch
instead leaves the field untouched and negates the $y$ component of the <b>mean unit vector
only</b>, where the azimuth is reported (§3.5) — the single place a sign convention is
observable.
<p style="margin-bottom:0">Confining it there is what makes the spatial operators correct.
Negating a component without reversing its axis yields a field belonging to no frame at all:
for any subsequent derivative $\partial v_y/\partial y = -\,\partial v_y/\partial(j\Delta y)$,
which is why confining the convention to the reported direction keeps §3.8 and §3.9 correct.</p></div>

<h3>3.2 &nbsp; Window selection and the validity mask</h3>

<p>A requested centre $c$ survives only if $\lfloor W/2\rfloor \le c \le N_T-1-\lfloor W/2\rfloor$.
Since the selector (0.2) always includes $0$ and $N_T-1$, <b>the first and last three
timepoints of any series never produce flow</b>, and a series shorter than $W$ skips the branch
entirely — returning all-$\mathrm{NaN}$ results with flag digit 4 <i>unset</i>.</p>

<p>Two filters define the per-voxel validity mask $\mathcal{M}$: a reliability percentile
$P$ (default $50$, so half the voxels are discarded) and, optionally, the segmentation:</p>

$$\mathcal{M}=\big\{\,\mathbf{x} : r(\mathbf{x})\ge Q_P(r)\,\big\}\ \cap\ \big\{\,\mathbf{x}\in \text{mask}\,\big\} \tag{3.5}$$

<p>where $Q_P$ is the $P^{\text{th}}$ percentile. The mask is applied differently by metric
type, which is the crux of the design: <b>pointwise</b> metrics use the masked field;
<b>spatial-derivative</b> metrics are evaluated on the full field and masked only at the
reduction, since puncturing the field before differentiating would make the derivative describe
the hole pattern; and the <b>correlation length</b> ignores both filters entirely.</p>

<h3>3.3 &nbsp; Mean speed $v$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Speed</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$</div>

$$v=\Big\langle\ \frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M}}
\sqrt{v_x^2+v_y^2+v_z^2}\ \Big\rangle_{\text{windows}} \tag{3.6}$$

<p>Genuinely 3D: out-of-plane motion contributes. A fully masked window yields $\mathrm{NaN}$
and is excluded from the average rather than counted as zero.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Speeds are a lower bound.</b> The solver is gradient-based, so recovered
speed is a fixed fraction of true speed when features are comparable in size to $\sigma_{xyz}$:
measured gain $\approx0.55$ at a 2-voxel feature scale and $\approx0.92$ at 6 voxels, at the
default $\sigma_{xyz}=3$. The gain is linear in speed, so relative comparisons across a dataset
are sound while absolute values are not.</div>

<h3>3.4 &nbsp; Speed change $\Delta v$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Speed Change</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}\,\mathrm{s}^{-1}$</div>
<p>The difference form of (0.6) over <i>windows</i>. $\mathrm{NaN}$ for a single window — and
because the default stride plus the edge exclusion frequently leaves exactly one surviving
centre, $\mathrm{NaN}$ here is common and expected.</p>

<h3>3.5 &nbsp; Flow direction $\theta$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mean Flow Direction</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> rad, range $(-\pi,\pi]$</div>
<div class="note">This is the <b>$xy$ azimuth only</b>. Elevation is deliberately discarded —
folded into §3.6 instead — so that the column remains comparable with 2D runs. There is no
elevation or polar-angle column.</div>
<p>Two-stage vector averaging, never an angle average. Per window the field is normalised to
3D unit vectors and averaged componentwise over valid voxels; the mean unit vector is
<i>not</i> renormalised, so its magnitude carries the coherence:</p>

$$\overline{\mathbf{e}}=\frac{1}{|\mathcal{M}|}\sum_{\mathbf{x}\in\mathcal{M}}
\frac{\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x})}{\|\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x})\|},
\qquad
\theta=\operatorname{atan2}\Big(-\big\langle \overline{e}_y\big\rangle,\ \big\langle \overline{e}_x\big\rangle\Big) \tag{3.7}$$

<p class="eqnote">The minus sign in (3.7) is the whole of the $y$-up convention (§3.1). It
cannot disturb a derivative, and $\sigma_\theta$ below takes the magnitude of
$\overline{\mathbf{e}}$, which the sign leaves unchanged.</p>

<p><b>Versus 2D.</b> The unit vectors here are 3D, so a voxel moving largely out of plane
contributes a shorter in-plane projection and its azimuth is damped, whereas 2D unit vectors
always have in-plane length exactly $1$. Further, 2D averages over <i>every</i> pixel including
motionless ones — where $\operatorname{atan2}(0,0)=0$ contributes $(1,0)$ and biases the result
toward $+x$ — while this averages over valid voxels only.</p>

<h3>3.6 &nbsp; Directional spread $\sigma_\theta$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Directional Spread</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> rad</div>
<p>Spherical dispersion from the resultant length of the <i>full 3D</i> unit vectors, so
out-of-plane scatter widens it where §3.5 is purely azimuthal:</p>

$$R=\big\|\overline{\mathbf{e}}\big\|,
\qquad
\sigma_\theta=\Big\langle \sqrt{-2\ln \tilde R}\ \Big\rangle,
\qquad \tilde R=\min\!\big(\max(R,10^{-12}),\,1\big) \tag{3.8}$$

<p>$R\to1$ is coherent flow ($\sigma_\theta\to0$); $R\to0$ is isotropic scatter. The clip bounds
$\sigma_\theta$ at $\approx7.434$ rad and makes a perfectly coherent window read exactly zero.</p>
<p class="eqnote">The resultant uses a NaN-<i>propagating</i> sum by design: a NaN-tolerant sum
would give $R=0$ for a fully masked window, which reads as perfectly isotropic flow and yields
a large but finite spread for a window containing no data at all. The 2D branch is unclipped,
so $R=0$ gives $+\infty$ and an $R$ marginally above $1$ gives $\mathrm{NaN}$.</p>

<h3>3.7 &nbsp; Velocity correlation length $\xi_v$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Velocity Correlation Length</span>
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}$</div>
<p>Evaluated on the <i>full, unmasked, unfiltered</i> field. Summing the Wiener–Khinchin
transform over the three components and normalising by the mean square speed,</p>

$$C(\mathbf{\Delta})=\frac{\big\langle \mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x})\cdot\mathbf{v}(\mathbf{x}+\mathbf{\Delta})\big\rangle}
{\big\langle \|\mathbf{v}\|^{2}\big\rangle}
=\frac{1}{\Nv\,\langle\|\mathbf{v}\|^2\rangle}\sum_{a\in\{x,y,z\}}
\mathcal{F}^{-1}\Big[\big|\mathcal{F}\{v_a\}\big|^{2}\Big] \tag{3.9}$$

<p>with $C(\mathbf{0})=1$. The radial averaging of (1.16) and the threshold-crossing rule of
(1.17) are then applied verbatim, with $\tau=0.5$ in place of $e^{-1}$, so the same divergence
from the reference release applies. $\xi_v=\langle\xi_v(i)\rangle$ over windows.</p>
<p>A window is $\mathrm{NaN}$ when the field is identically zero, when $r_{\max}\le\delta$, or
when $C$ never crosses $0.5$ within $r_{\max}$. <b>Any</b> of the three raises flag digit 4,
not only the last.</p>
<p class="eqnote">Because (3.9) depends on each component only through $|\mathcal{F}\{v_a\}|^2$,
it is invariant under $v_a\to-v_a$ — which is why $\xi_v$ escapes the defect of (3.4).</p>

<h3>3.8 &nbsp; Divergence $\nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{v}$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Divergence</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in a volumetric mode; blank in 2D (§6, D6)</div>

$$\nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{v}=\frac{\partial v_x}{\partial x}+\frac{\partial v_y}{\partial y}
+\frac{\partial v_z}{\partial z} \tag{3.10}$$

<p>by second-order central differences on the physical grid (one-sided at the borders),
evaluated on the full field, masked at the reduction, and averaged over windows. Positive
indicates local expansion.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Verified against analytic fields:</b> $
abla\!\cdot\!(k\mathbf{r})=3k$ exactly, zero for uniform flow and zero for rigid rotation, and invariant under proper rotations. The field reaches this operator in the solver's own consistent frame (§3.1).</div>
<p><b>Versus 2D.</b> Not the same quantity under any correction. The 2D metric is the spatial
mean divergence of the <i>cumulative sum of unit-vector fields</i>, taken at the last frame
pair only; it therefore describes accumulated direction rather than velocity, carries units of
$\mu\mathrm{m}^{-1}$, and depends on how many pairs preceded it.</p>

<h3>3.9 &nbsp; Curl $\Omega$</h3>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Curl</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mathrm{s}^{-1}$ in a volumetric mode; blank in 2D (§6, D6)</div>
<p>Curl is a vector in three dimensions and there is one column, so the branch reports the mean
of its <i>magnitude</i>:</p>

$$\nabla\!\times\!\mathbf{v}=
\Big(\partial_y v_z-\partial_z v_y,\ \ \partial_z v_x-\partial_x v_z,\ \ \partial_x v_y-\partial_y v_x\Big),
\qquad
\Omega=\Big\langle \big\|\nabla\!\times\!\mathbf{v}\big\| \Big\rangle \tag{3.11}$$

<div class="note">Being a magnitude, $\Omega\ge0$ and carries <b>no handedness</b>: clockwise
and anticlockwise vortices give the same value, and counter-rotating regions do not cancel
in the mean — they add. The 2D metric is a signed scalar, where they do cancel.</div>
<div class="note"><b>Verified against analytic fields:</b> $\|
abla\!	imes\!\mathbf{v}\|=2\omega$ everywhere for solid-body rotation at rate $\omega$, zero for uniform flow, and invariant under proper rotations. (A plain transpose is a <i>reflection</i>, which flips the curl sign, so it is not a valid check.)</div>

<h2 class="branch">4 &nbsp; Volumetric Branch</h2>
<p>Sections 1–3 are the <b>three branches</b> of published BARCODE, restated for a volume. This is
the fourth branch: everything the volume makes possible that a flat image cannot give you. It is
one branch, not seven — the families inside it are grouped by <b>what they require</b>, because
that is the only thing to decide before a run. Almost all are <b>opt-in</b>:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Family</th><th>§</th><th>Default</th><th>Needs a segmentation?</th><th>Also needs</th></tr>
<tr><td>Surface mesh</td><td>4.1–4.10</td><td>off</td><td><b>no</b> — falls back to the thresholded volume</td><td>isotropic grid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Curvature</td><td>4.11–4.16</td><td>on within meshing</td><td>no (rides on the mesh)</td><td>the mesh</td></tr>
<tr><td>Object size distribution</td><td>4.17</td><td>off</td><td>no</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Depth profile</td><td>4.18</td><td>off</td><td>no</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>In-mask intensity</td><td>4.19</td><td>off</td><td><b>yes</b></td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Packing topology</td><td>4.20</td><td>off</td><td><b>instance</b> mask</td><td>isotropic grid</td></tr>
<tr><td>Object rows</td><td>4.21</td><td>by <span class="mono">row_axis</span></td><td><b>instance</b> mask</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td>Range provenance</td><td>4.22</td><td>off</td><td>no</td><td>—</td></tr>
</table>
<div class="note"><b>Sixteen of these columns need cubic voxels and are absent without them</b> —
the whole mesh and curvature set, and all of packing. &sect;P.4 lists which metrics depend on the
grid and what to do about each.</div>
<p><b>A single volume is a normal input.</b> Every <i>Change</i> metric is then NaN — a change
needs two points on the progression axis — and everything else is live.</p>

<h3 class="grp">Runs without a segmentation</h3>
<p><b>Surface mesh.</b></p>

<p>Nine columns describing a triangulated surface. Requires an isotropic grid; opt-in, though
the <span class="mono">xyzt</span> schema advertises the columns. A <b>missing segmentation no
longer prevents meshing</b> — the branch falls back to the <i>binarized volume</i> and says so,
which is a threshold surface rather than the object. Surface extraction is a CGAL
Delaunay-refinement mesher at <span class="mono">mesh_isovalue</span> (default $0.5$) on the
binary volume, with radius bound <span class="mono">mesh_maxrad</span>; there is deliberately
no marching-cubes fallback, which would produce meshes not comparable with the reference
MATLAB pipeline. The binary is reduced to its largest 26-connected component and padded by one
voxel of background so a boundary-touching object still closes; the mesh — not the mask — is
then smoothed by Laplacian-HC.</p>

<div class="note"><b>The isovalue is the boundary a binary mask means.</b> <span class="mono">mesh_isovalue</span> defaults to $0.5$ — the boundary between the last foreground voxel and the first background one, and the most accurate value on rounded objects (within ~0.1% of the exact sphere volume down to a 16-voxel radius). A higher value pulls the surface inward onto foreground-voxel centres and shrinks small objects; setting $0.99$ with <span class="mono">mesh_matlab_compat</span> reproduces the MATLAB pipeline. The MATLAB-agreement figures below describe $0.99$.</div>

<p>Let the mesh be $(\mathcal{V},\mathcal{T})$ with vertices $\mathbf{p}\in\R^3$ in
micrometres and triangles $t=(\mathbf{p}_0,\mathbf{p}_1,\mathbf{p}_2)$.</p>

<h4>4.1 &nbsp; Mesh volume $V_m$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mesh Volume</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$</div>
<p>By the divergence theorem, as a sum of signed tetrahedra on the origin:</p>

$$V_m=\Big|\ \frac{1}{6}\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}}
\mathbf{p}_0\cdot(\mathbf{p}_1\times\mathbf{p}_2)\ \Big| \tag{4.1}$$

<p>The sign encodes surface orientation and is retained as a diagnostic. $V_m$ is deliberately
distinct from $I_{tot}^{\mathcal Q}$ of §1.9: meshing smooths the surface, so the two differ by
a few percent, and §4.7 reports exactly that discrepancy.</p>

<h4>4.2 &nbsp; Mesh surface area $A_m$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mesh Surface Area</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}^2$</div>

$$A_m=\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}} a_t,
\qquad
a_t=\tfrac12\big\|(\mathbf{p}_1-\mathbf{p}_0)\times(\mathbf{p}_2-\mathbf{p}_0)\big\| \tag{4.2}$$

<h4>4.3 &nbsp; Sphericity $\Psi$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Sphericity</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless</div>
<p>Wadell sphericity — the area of a sphere of equal volume over the actual area. Exactly $1$
for a sphere, below $1$ otherwise; $\mathrm{NaN}$ when $A_m\le0$.</p>

$$\Psi=\frac{\pi^{1/3}\big(6V_m\big)^{2/3}}{A_m} \tag{4.3}$$

<h4>4.4 &nbsp; Equivalent sphere radius $R_{eq}$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Equivalent Sphere Radius</span>
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}$</div>

$$R_{eq}=\Big(\frac{3V_m}{4\pi}\Big)^{1/3} \tag{4.4}$$

<h4>4.5 &nbsp; Mesh height $h$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mesh Height</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}$</div>
<p>The $z$ extent of the face <i>centroids</i> $\mathbf{q}_t=\tfrac13(\mathbf{p}_0+\mathbf{p}_1+\mathbf{p}_2)$,
not of the vertices:</p>

$$h=\max_{t}\,q_{t,z}-\min_{t}\,q_{t,z} \tag{4.5}$$

<p class="eqnote">Deliberate parity with the reference MATLAB implementation. Centroids lie
inside the surface, so $h$ is systematically shorter than the true $z$ extent — which is the
sole reason a perfect sphere reports a lateral/axial ratio near $1.08$ rather than $1$.</p>

<h4>4.6 &nbsp; Lateral/Axial ratio $\alpha$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Lateral/Axial Ratio</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless</div>
<p>Projecting the largest connected component along $z$ and fitting the ellipse with the same
normalised second central moments, with major and minor axes
$\ell_{\text{maj}},\ell_{\text{min}}$:</p>

$$\alpha=\frac{\ell_{\text{maj}}+\ell_{\text{min}}}{2h} \tag{4.6}$$

<p>Lateral size over axial size: $\alpha>1$ is oblate, $\alpha\approx1$ round. Dimensionless
and hence size-independent. $\mathrm{NaN}$ if $h=0$ or the mask is absent.</p>

<h4>4.7 &nbsp; Mesh volume ratio $\Upsilon$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mesh Volume Ratio</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless</div>
<p>The mesh volume against the raw voxel-counted volume of the same mask, where $n_{\text{obj}}$
is the object's voxel count:</p>

$$\Upsilon=\frac{V_m}{n_{\text{obj}}\,v_0} \tag{4.7}$$

<div class="note"><b>A fidelity check, not a shape descriptor.</b> $\Upsilon\approx1$ means the
mesh faithfully represents the mask. The default radius bound is tuned for objects
$\sim200$ voxels across and loses roughly half the volume of a thin object: measured
$\Upsilon = 0.51,\,0.73,\,0.78,\,0.80,\,0.81$ at
<span class="mono">mesh_maxrad</span> $=5.0,\,3.0,\,2.0,\,1.5,\,1.0$. Scale the bound to the
object's <i>thinnest</i> dimension, roughly depth$/8$, and check $\Upsilon$ on any dataset that
is not a nucleus.</div>

<h4>4.8 &nbsp; Solidity $\Sigma$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Solidity</span>
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> dimensionless</div>
<p>Both sides are <i>voxel counts</i> — deliberately, so that meshing shrinkage does not
contaminate what is meant to be a pure convexity measure. With $\mathcal{H}$ the convex hull
taken over voxel centres,</p>

$$\Sigma=\frac{n_{\text{obj}}}{n_{\mathcal{H}}} \tag{4.8}$$

<p>$\Sigma=1$ is convex, lower is more lobed. Degenerate inputs (fewer than four object voxels,
or a hull failure on collinear or planar input) report the object as fully solid.</p>

<h4>4.9 &nbsp; Concavity — removed</h4>
<p>There is <b>no</b> <span class="mono">Concavity</span> column. It was $1-\Sigma$ and carried
no information Solidity does not, so it was dropped from every schema — the field mesh family
and the per-object rows alike. Read lobedness directly off <span class="mono">Solidity</span>
(§4.8): lower is more lobed. For a genuine surface-concavity measure use
<span class="mono">Concave Area Fraction</span> (§4.14), a different quantity entirely — the area
fraction of concave faces, not a hull-fill ratio.</p>
<p class="eqnote">The voxel-centre convention runs $\approx1.5\%$ above the reference MATLAB
column by construction; it was chosen for exact agreement with a sibling analysis package
rather than with MATLAB. This is a source-comment claim, not pinned by a test in this repo.</p>

<h4>4.10 &nbsp; Aggregation</h4>
<p>One object is meshed per analysed timepoint — the largest connected component — and every
mesh field is reduced by the finite-only mean (0.3) over timepoints. Note this averages the
<i>extensive</i> quantities $V_m$ and $A_m$ as well as the intensive ones. Per-object meshing of
a labelled field is implemented but has no pipeline call site; see defect D3.</p>

<p><b>Curvature.</b></p>

<p>Principal curvatures over the mesh surface, estimated by finite differences of vertex normals
along triangle edges, solved per face in least squares, projected into per-vertex tangent frames
and accumulated with mixed-Voronoi area weights — neither a quadric fit nor a cotangent
Laplacian. Verified bit-for-bit against the reference MATLAB toolbox.</p>

<p>Winding is corrected first: if (4.1) is signed negative the faces are flipped, since an
inward-wound mesh would invert every sign. Per-vertex principal curvatures are averaged to their
three corners, giving per-face $\kappa_{\min,t}\le\kappa_{\max,t}$ and</p>

$$H_t=\tfrac12\big(\kappa_{\min,t}+\kappa_{\max,t}\big),
\qquad
\mathcal{K}_t=\kappa_{\min,t}\,\kappa_{\max,t} \tag{5.1}$$

<p>the pointwise mean and Gaussian curvatures. Units are $\mu\mathrm{m}^{-1}$; with outward
normals a sphere of radius $R$ gives $H=+1/R$, so <b>concave is negative</b>. $\mathcal{K}_t$ is
computed but never emitted.</p>

<h4>4.11 &nbsp; Which faces are excluded — by default, none</h4>
<p><b>By default no faces are excluded — curvature is measured over the whole surface, and top
and bottom caps are not clipped.</b> Two exclusion rules exist, both <b>off by default</b>, and
both must be switched on deliberately with a supplied threshold — heavily gated because
discarding surface changes every curvature number:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Setting</th><th>Default</th><th>Excludes</th></tr>
<tr><td><span class="mono">curvature_exclude_caps</span></td><td><span class="mono">False</span></td>
<td>Faces whose centroid falls in the lowest or highest $0.1\,\mu\mathrm{m}$ $z$ bin.</td></tr>
<tr><td><span class="mono">curvature_outlier_limit</span></td><td><span class="mono">0.0</span> (off)</td>
<td>Faces with $|H|$ above the limit, in $\mu\mathrm{m}^{-1}$. MATLAB uses $2.0$, a radius of curvature below $0.5\,\mu\mathrm{m}$.</td></tr>
</table>
<p>The two answer different questions. <b>Cap exclusion is anatomical</b>: where a segmentation
is clipped by the ends of the imaged stack the resulting flat cap is an artefact of the
acquisition, not a surface of the object. Turn it on when flag digit 6 is raised. For an object
sitting entirely inside the volume it discards real surface, which is why it is off by default. <b>The outlier limit is numerical</b>: a face reporting a radius of curvature
well below the mesh's own edge length is a badly conditioned triangle rather than fine
structure. With the default of $0$ every face counts, so a mesh with degenerate triangles can
pull $\langle H
angle$ around — check Mesh Volume Ratio (§4.7) if a value looks implausible.</p>
<p>When a limit is set the outlier decision is made <b>once, on $H$</b>, and reused for the
minimum and maximum, so all three means always run over an identical face set.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Reproducing MATLAB.</b>
Setting <span class="mono">curvature_exclude_caps = True</span> and
<span class="mono">curvature_outlier_limit = 2.0</span> reproduces the MATLAB behaviour and the
parity recorded in §4.11. The defaults measure the whole surface — the more defensible starting
point for an object fully inside the stack — so discarding surface is opt-in.</div>
<p>With both exclusion rules off, the two ratios (§4.13, §4.14) and the three means share a single
denominator — the whole surface, less any face whose principal curvatures were non-finite.</p>

<h4>4.12 &nbsp; Mean curvature $\langle H\rangle$</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Column</b> <span class="mono">Mean Curvature &lt;H&gt;</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}^{-1}$</div>

$$\langle H\rangle=\frac{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}^{\ast}} a_t H_t}{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}^{\ast}} a_t} \tag{5.3}$$

<p>Two averages are stacked: $H$ is already a <i>local</i> mean of the two principal curvatures,
and (4.10) then averages it over the usable surface — hence the $\langle\cdot\rangle$ notation
rather than the bare name. $\mathrm{NaN}$ when the usable area is zero.</p>

<h4>4.13–4.14 &nbsp; Invagination ratio and concave area fraction</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Invagination Ratio</span>,
<span class="mono">Concave Area Fraction</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless, $0\!-\!1$</div>
<p>Faces are classified in this precedence — note $\kappa_{\max}\le0$ wins, so a face with both
curvatures zero is concave:</p>

$$\text{class}(t)=\begin{cases}
\text{concave}, & \kappa_{\max,t}\le 0\\
\text{saddle}, & \kappa_{\max,t}>0 \ \text{and}\ \kappa_{\min,t}\le 0\\
\text{convex}, & \text{otherwise}
\end{cases} \tag{5.4}$$

<p>and with $\mathcal{D}=\{t\notin\mathcal{T}_{bt}\}$ as the common denominator,</p>

$$\text{Invagination}=\frac{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{D},\ \text{concave or saddle}} a_t}{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{D}} a_t},
\qquad
\text{Concave}=\frac{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{D},\ \text{concave}} a_t}{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{D}} a_t} \tag{5.5}$$

<p>The concave fraction is a strict subset of the invagination ratio, so it is always the
smaller of the two.</p>

<h4>4.15–4.16 &nbsp; Minimum and maximum curvature</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Minimum Curvature</span>,
<span class="mono">Maximum Curvature</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> $\mu\mathrm{m}^{-1}$ &nbsp;·&nbsp; opt-in</div>

$$\kappa_{\min}=\frac{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}^{\ast}} a_t\,\kappa_{\min,t}}{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}^{\ast}} a_t},
\qquad
\kappa_{\max}=\frac{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}^{\ast}} a_t\,\kappa_{\max,t}}{\sum_{t\in\mathcal{T}^{\ast}} a_t} \tag{5.6}$$

<p>These are area-weighted <i>means</i> of the per-face principal curvatures, not global extrema.
They exist because (4.10) averages $\kappa_{\min}$ and $\kappa_{\max}$ together, so a saddle —
sharply curved in both directions — averages towards zero and reads as flat. A surface can be
highly structured and still report $\langle H\rangle\approx0$; (4.13) says how structured.</p>

<h4>4.17 &nbsp; Object size distribution</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Island Count</span>,
<span class="mono">Island Volume SD</span>, <span class="mono">Island Volume Skewness</span>,
<span class="mono">Median Island Volume</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp;
<b>Unit</b> dimensionless &nbsp;·&nbsp; opt-in, volumetric only</div>
<p>The binarization branch gives the largest, mean and total object size but cannot say whether
objects are uniform or wildly unequal — one dominant object plus debris has the same mean as
several even ones. Writing $s_k=|\mathcal{R}_k(i)|$ and $\bar s_i$ for their mean,</p>

$$n=\big\langle n_i\big\rangle,
\qquad
\text{SD}=\frac{1}{\Nv}\Big\langle \big[\tfrac{1}{n_i}\textstyle\sum_k (s_k-\bar s_i)^2\big]^{1/2}\Big\rangle,
\qquad
\text{Med}=\frac{1}{\Nv}\big\langle \operatorname{median}_k s_k \big\rangle \tag{6.1}$$

$$\text{Skew}=\Big\langle \frac{1}{n_i}\sum_k\Big(\frac{s_k-\bar s_i}{\sigma_{s,i}}\Big)^{3} \Big\rangle \tag{6.2}$$

<p>Standard deviations are the population form. The skewness is $\mathrm{NaN}$ unless a frame
holds more than two objects with non-zero variance; the SD is $0$, not $\mathrm{NaN}$, for a
single object. All four are means of per-timepoint statistics, <i>not</i> statistics pooled over
timepoints.</p>

<h4>4.18 &nbsp; Depth profile</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Maximal Area Slice Index</span>,
<span class="mono">Maximal Area Slice Depth</span>, <span class="mono">Maximal Area Slice Area</span>
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Units</b> slice, $\mu\mathrm{m}$, fraction of FOV &nbsp;·&nbsp; opt-in</div>
<p>The only metrics that say <i>where</i> in depth something is, rather than reducing the stack
to one number. With $a_z$ the foreground fraction of slice $z$,</p>

$$a_z=\frac{1}{n_x n_y}\sum_{x,y} b(x,y,z,i),
\qquad
z^{\ast}=\arg\max_z a_z,
\qquad
d^{\ast}=z^{\ast}\Delta z \tag{8.1}$$

<p>reported as $\langle z^\ast\rangle$, $\langle d^\ast\rangle$ and $\langle a_{z^\ast}\rangle$.
The first maximum wins on a tie, and the time-average makes the reported index fractional. The
index is into the <i>analysed</i> stack, and $d^{\ast}$ is measured from the first analysed
slice — a shape descriptor, not an absolute stage position.</p>
<p>This family also raises <b>flag digit 6</b>, the union over analysed timepoints of whether
foreground touches any of the six faces of the analysed volume. When it fires, every size,
shape and curvature metric describes a truncated object, which is not recoverable from the
numbers themselves.</p>

<h3 class="grp">Requires a segmentation</h3>
<h4>4.19 &nbsp; In-mask intensity</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">In-Mask MFI</span>,
<span class="mono">SD</span>, <span class="mono">CV</span>, <span class="mono">Skewness</span>,
<span class="mono">Entropy</span>, <span class="mono">Normalized Entropy</span>,
<span class="mono">Fraction Above 2x Median</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; opt-in, requires a segmentation</div>
<p>How signal is distributed <i>inside</i> each object — the clustering readout. The intensity
branch describes whatever voxels it is given, usually including background; a uniformly filled
nucleus and one with bright foci share a mean and differ sharply in CV and entropy.</p>
<p>For object $o$ with raw voxel values $\{u\}$, mean $\mu_o$ and population SD $\sigma_o$:</p>

$$\mathrm{CV}_o=\frac{\sigma_o}{\mu_o},
\qquad
\gamma_o=\frac{1}{|o|}\sum_{u}\Big(\frac{u-\mu_o}{\sigma_o}\Big)^{3},
\qquad
\Phi^{2\times}_o=\frac{\big|\{u : u>2\operatorname{median}(u)\}\big|}{|o|} \tag{9.1}$$

<p>Entropy alone uses a per-object rescaling $\tilde u=(u-\min u)/(\max u-\min u)$, binned into
$N_e$ bins (default $64$) over the <i>fixed</i> range $[0,1]$ so that objects share bin widths:</p>

$$S_o=-\sum_{j:\,q_j>0} q_j\log_2 q_j,
\qquad
\hat S_o=\frac{S_o}{\log_2 N_e} \tag{9.2}$$

<p>Base 2, so the unit is bits with maximum $\log_2 64=6$. $\hat S_o=1$ is a uniformly filled
object, falling toward $0$ as signal concentrates. Runs are comparable only at equal $N_e$,
since it sets both the achievable entropy and the denominator.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Only entropy is rescaled — deliberately.</b> The source MATLAB rescales
before every statistic. CV and skewness are already scale-invariant and the $-\min$ shift of an
affine map distorts them; and the bright fraction degenerates entirely, since a punctate
object's rescaled median is $0$, exactly where the metric matters most.</div>
<p>Objects below <span class="mono">mask_intensity_min_voxels</span> (default $8$) are skipped,
as is any object of constant value. Every statistic is the <b>unweighted</b> mean over objects,
then over timepoints, so one large object does not outvote the rest. CV and the bright fraction
are $\mathrm{NaN}$ when the object's mean or median is not positive; skewness needs at least
three voxels and non-zero variance.</p>

<h4>4.20 &nbsp; Packing topology</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Mean Contact Number</span>,
<span class="mono">Contact Number SD</span>, <span class="mono">Hexagonal Fraction</span>
&nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> dimensionless &nbsp;·&nbsp; opt-in, requires an instance segmentation</div>
<div class="warn"><span class="h">Requires an isotropic grid; left empty without one</span>
Contact is counted in <b>voxel faces</b> and gaps bridged by a <b>voxel dilation</b>. On a
non-cubic grid a $z$-normal face spans a different physical area from an $xy$-normal one, so
the same physical interface passes or fails the threshold on its orientation alone. Unlike
Anisotropy (§1.7), which had the same class of bug and was <i>fixed</i> by measuring in
physical coordinates, no rescaling repairs this. Above a 1% anisotropy ratio the family is
left empty and its columns never reach the CSV or barcode. The general rule: <b>a metric
defined only on a cubic grid must not reach the output.</b></div>
<p>Nothing else in BARCODE describes the <i>topology</i> of a packing. In a space-filling
monolayer sizes and separations are near-uniform and it is the neighbour-number distribution
that changes. The family refuses rather than falling back: with a boolean mask, connectivity
labelling would fuse a confluent field into one component and report a contact number of zero,
which reads as a measurement rather than a misconfiguration.</p>
<p>Labels are first dilated by $d$ voxels to bridge thin background gaps. Two labels are
adjacent if they meet across a shared <i>face</i> — 6-connectivity, so edge- and corner-touching
objects are deliberately not neighbours — and the pair survives only if the shared face count
reaches $m$ (default $5$, floored at $1$, so $m=0$ does not disable the filter):</p>

$$E=\Big\{\{u,w\}\ :\ u\neq w,\ \ \big|\{\mathbf{x}\ \text{face-adjacent}\ :\ \{L(\mathbf{x}),L(\mathbf{x}')\}=\{u,w\}\}\big|\ \ge m\Big\} \tag{7.1}$$

<p>The contact number of a label is its degree in $(\,\text{labels},E)$ — a count of distinct
neighbours, not a contact area. Objects reaching the edge are excluded from
the <i>reported set</i> $\mathcal{I}$, though their neighbours still count toward interior
degrees. Which faces count is set by <span class="mono">packing_border_mode</span>, default
<b>"xy"</b> — the four lateral faces only, because in a thin slab every object touches the top
and bottom and the six-face rule ("all") would exclude the entire field. Then</p>
<div class="note"><b>The family assumes an isotropic grid.</b> Both
<span class="mono">packing_contact_dilation_vox</span> and
<span class="mono">packing_min_contact_voxels</span> count voxels, weighting a $z$-normal
contact face like an $xy$-normal one. At the 4.6× anisotropy of a typical acquisition the
same physical contact passes or fails the threshold according to its orientation alone.</div>

$$\bar{\mathcal{C}}=\Big\langle \tfrac{1}{|\mathcal{I}|}\textstyle\sum_{u\in\mathcal{I}}\deg u \Big\rangle,
\qquad
\Phi_{6}=\Big\langle \tfrac{1}{|\mathcal{I}|}\big|\{u\in\mathcal{I}:\deg u=6\}\big| \Big\rangle \tag{7.2}$$

<p>with the SD the population form over $\mathcal{I}$. All three are $\mathrm{NaN}$, with a
stated reason, when fewer than two objects exist or when every object touches the border.
$\Phi_6$ is the more diagnostic of the two: in any space-filling 2D tiling the mean degree is
pinned near $6$ by topology, while the fraction is not.</p>

<h3 class="grp">Per object</h3>
<h4>4.21 &nbsp; Object rows</h4>
<p>When <span class="mono">row_axis</span> resolves to <span class="mono">object</span>, a second and much smaller schema is written to <span class="mono">&lt;name&gt; Objects.csv</span> — one row per segmented object: 10 base columns (volume, a principal-axis anisotropy needing no mesh, contact number and the seven in-mask statistics) plus five per-object mesh-shape columns when <span class="mono">object_mesh</span> is on. See <span class="mono">docs/row_axes.md</span>.</p>
<h3 class="grp">Recording what was analysed</h3>
<h4>4.22 &nbsp; Range provenance</h4>
<div class="meta"><b>Columns</b> <span class="mono">Z Range Start/End</span>,
<span class="mono">T Range Start/End</span> &nbsp;·&nbsp; <b>Unit</b> slice &nbsp;·&nbsp; opt-in</div>
<p>Flag digit 5 records <i>that</i> the analysis covered part of the data; these record which
part, and make per-file ranges representable where a global setting cannot. Both ends are
inclusive, matching the settings a user typed. An unrestricted axis reports its full extent
rather than $\mathrm{NaN}$ — "$0$ to $53$" and "no range set" are the same statement. Indices are into the
<i>acquired</i> data, before any isotropic resampling; the two time columns carry the unit label
"slice", which is a mild misnomer.</p>

<h2 class="branch">Column index — look up what you have</h2>
<p>Every column BARCODE can emit, in every mode and both unit systems, with the section
that defines it. Use this when holding a CSV rather than reading front to back.</p>
<p>Two things this makes visible that the per-metric entries do not. <b>The same
measurement changes name between modes</b> — <span class="mono">Maximum Island Area</span>
in <span class="mono">xyt</span>/<span class="mono">xyz</span> is <span class="mono">Maximum
Island Volume</span> in <span class="mono">xyzt</span>, and the five Change metrics gain an
<span class="mono">(over Z)</span> suffix in <span class="mono">xyz</span> because their
progression axis is depth, not time. And <b>every size metric appears twice</b>: a bare name
holding a fraction of the analysed field, and a <span class="mono">… Quantity</span> name
holding a physical size in $\mu\mathrm{m}^2$ or $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$. A given CSV carries one
set or the other, never both.</p>
<p>The table lists <b>metrics</b> only. Every CSV also opens with three identity
columns — <span class="mono">File</span>, <span class="mono">Channel</span> and
<span class="mono">Flags</span> — described in §4.11–4.16.</p>
