<h2 class="branch">Reading the outputs</h2>
<div class="note"><b>What a row is, is a setting.</b> <span class="mono">row_axis</span>
chooses between one row per file, timepoint, z-slice or <b>object</b>, and the barcode
normalises per column <i>across rows</i> — so it decides what the picture compares. An
<span class="mono">object</span> axis emits a second schema (10 base columns —
<span class="mono">Object Volume</span>, a principal-axis <b>anisotropy</b> computed without a
mesh, contact number and the in-mask statistics — plus five per-object mesh-shape columns when
<span class="mono">object_mesh</span>
is on) to <span class="mono">&lt;name&gt; Objects.csv</span>. See <span class="mono">docs/row_axes.md</span>
and §4.45 of the markdown reference.</div>

<h3>What a volumetric run writes</h3>
<p>A dataset-level <span class="mono">&lt;name&gt; Summary.csv</span>, a
<span class="mono">&lt;name&gt; Summary Barcode.png</span>, and a
<span class="mono">&lt;name&gt; Settings.yaml</span> recording the configuration, in the folder
processed. Several harnesses also write a <span class="mono">… (physical).csv</span> carrying
the $\mu\mathrm{m}^2$/$\mu\mathrm{m}^3$ Quantity columns beside the normalised one.</p>
<p><b>There are no per-file outputs in the volumetric modes</b> — no reduced data structures,
per-frame graphs or per-file figures. Enabling meshing with OBJ export additionally writes
<span class="mono">&lt;stem&gt; BARCODE Meshes/</span> beside the input.</p>
<div class="note"><b>Keep the Settings.yaml.</b> Several metrics are interpretable only against
the settings that produced them — normalized entropy depends on the bin count, correlation
lengths on the voxel size, Speed on the frame interval — and the CSV does not carry those
values. <span class="mono">record_range_columns</span> embeds at least the analysed z/t ranges
into the CSV itself (§4.22).</div>

<h3>Reading the barcode PNG</h3>
<p>Rows are files or series, columns are metrics, colour is the value on the <b>plasma</b>
colormap. <b>NaN renders black</b> — worth knowing before reading a black cell as "low".
Scaling is per column, but how each column is scaled depends on its unit, and only some are
normalised against the data:</p>
<table>
<tr><th>Column type</th><th>Colour range</th></tr>
<tr><td><span class="mono">fraction of FOV</span>, <span class="mono">fraction of frames</span></td><td><b>fixed</b> $[0,1]$ — comparable between runs</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mean Flow Direction</td><td><b>fixed</b> $[-\pi,\pi]$</td></tr>
<tr><td>Directional Spread</td><td><b>fixed</b> $[0,\pi]$; larger values clipped in the image only</td></tr>
<tr><td>Mean Island Anisotropy</td><td>$[1,\max]$</td></tr>
<tr><td>ratio to initial</td><td>data range, always including $1$</td></tr>
<tr><td>Curvature, Divergence, Curl, Speed Change</td><td>data range, always including $0$ (signed)</td></tr>
<tr><td>Other physical units</td><td>$[0,\max]$</td></tr>
<tr><td>Remaining dimensionless</td><td>data range, always including $0$</td></tr>
</table>
<p>A column with a <b>fixed</b> range can be compared between two barcodes; one scaled to the
data cannot, since its extremes come from whichever rows are in that figure. Dynamic ranges are
taken across every rendered row, so <b>adding or removing files recolours those columns</b>.
Read the CSV when comparing across figures.</p>

<h2 class="branch">5 &nbsp; Warnings and flags</h2>

<div class="warn"><span class="h">Flags are emitted semicolon-joined</span>
A row with a restricted $z$ range whose structural correlation failed reads
<span class="mono">3;5</span>, not a single digit. <span class="mono">0</span> means no flag
fired. Any parser reading this column as an integer is wrong on multi-flag rows.</div>

<table>
<tr><th>Digit</th><th>Meaning</th><th>Criterion</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>0</b></td><td>No flag fired</td><td>—</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>1</b></td><td>Dim channel</td>
<td>$\tfrac{2}{e}\overline{B}\le B_{\min}$ — the darkest voxel is already a sizeable fraction of the average. Judged on the first frame in 2D and the first <i>analysed</i> volume in <span class="mono">xyzt</span>. Never fires in <span class="mono">xyz</span>.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>2</b></td><td>Saturation</td>
<td>The histogram mode occupies the highest surviving bin in <i>every</i> analysed timepoint. Makes §2.7 meaningless.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>3</b></td><td>Structural correlation failure</td>
<td>$\xi_I(i)=\mathrm{NaN}$ for <i>at least one</i> analysed timepoint.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>4</b></td><td>Velocity correlation failure</td>
<td>$\xi_v(i)=\mathrm{NaN}$ for at least one window. <b>Not</b> raised when the branch was skipped outright.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>5</b></td><td>Range restricted</td>
<td>A $z$ or $t$ range was applied — <i>you</i> narrowed the analysis.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>7</b></td><td>Open mesh surface</td>
<td>A mesh came out with a boundary rather than closed, so $V_m$, $\Psi$ and the curvature <i>signs</i> are unreliable for that row — an unclosed surface has no well-defined inside.</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>6</b></td><td>Field-of-view clipping</td>
<td>Foreground reaches a face of the analysed volume — the <i>data</i> is cut off.</td></tr>
</table>

<p>Digits 3 and 4 fire if <i>any single</i> analysed timepoint failed, the test being
NaN-propagating, whereas the metric itself is a NaN-dropping mean. A finite value with its flag
raised therefore means "most timepoints worked, at least one did not". Digit 3's documented
meaning is "the correlation length exceeds the field of view", which is the usual cause but not
the only one — an empty radial shell also blocks a crossing (§1.11).</p>

<h2 class="branch">6 &nbsp; Known limitations and caveats</h2>

<p>The current version has the following limitations. None changes how a metric is defined;
they are things to know when setting up a run or reading a result.</p>

<h4>Open issues</h4>
<table>
<tr><th></th><th>Issue</th><th>Effect</th></tr>
<tr><td>L1</td><td><span class="mono">perslice</span> histogram <span class="mono">IndexError</span> is unguarded</td>
<td>Fires when no bin clears the noise threshold — routine for a sparse in-mask selection, and the masked <span class="mono">xyz</span> path routes through it. Swallowed by the caller, so all six intensity columns read NaN while the run reports success.</td></tr>
<tr><td>L2</td><td>"Parse All Channels" notice fires once per process</td>
<td>A module-level flag never reset, so in the GUI every run after the first drops channels with no message.</td></tr>
<tr><td>L3</td><td>No test covers the pipeline-level flow sign convention</td>
<td>The flow tests call the divergence and curl operators directly on synthetic fields, so a regression in the field's sign handling would not be caught by CI.</td></tr>
<tr><td>L4</td><td>Resampler edge-clamping is unrecorded</td>
<td>Repeating the last plane past the mask overhang avoids injecting background, but nothing in the CSV or provenance records which resampling convention produced a file.</td></tr>
<tr><td>L5</td><td><span class="mono">packing_border_mode</span> and <span class="mono">row_axis</span> have no GUI control</td>
<td>Reachable only from a YAML or the CLI.</td></tr>
<tr><td>L6</td><td>z-range/mask mapping assumes node-aligned grids</td>
<td>A restricted range on a finer mask grid can be misregistered by up to about one acquired slice at the deep end.</td></tr>
</table>

<h4>Where 2D and 3D numbers must not be pooled</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Mean island separation</b> — 2D includes the self-distance, deflating it by $\kappa/(\kappa+1)$; (1.12) excludes it.</li>
<li><b>Divergence</b> — 2D is the divergence of a cumulative <i>unit-vector</i> field at the last frame pair ($\mu\mathrm{m}^{-1}$); 3D is a window-mean $\nabla\!\cdot\!\mathbf{v}$ ($\mathrm{s}^{-1}$).</li>
<li><b>Curl</b> — 2D is a signed scalar (opposite vortices cancel); 3D is an unsigned magnitude (they add).</li>
<li><b>Area / Volume Quantity</b> — the 2D "Area Quantity" is $\mu\mathrm{m}\!\cdot\!\mathrm{px}$; the 3D Volume Quantity (0.7) is a true $\mu\mathrm{m}^3$.</li>
</ul>

<h4>Measurement caveats</h4>
<ul>
<li><b>Flow speeds are a lower bound</b> (gain 0.55–0.92 by feature scale); relative comparisons within a dataset are sound, absolute values are not (§3.3).</li>
<li><b><span class="mono">flow_downsample</span> is a fixed dataset-wide choice</b>, not a performance dial — it changes Curl, Divergence and $\xi_v$ by factors of 0.4–2.3 (§3.1).</li>
<li><b>The velocity correlation length ignores the reliability filter and the mask</b>, unlike every other flow metric (§3.2).</li>
<li><b><span class="mono">mesh_maxrad</span> must be scaled to the object</b> — the default of 5 loses roughly half a thin object's volume; Mesh Volume Ratio (§4.7) is the detector.</li>
<li><b>Solidity runs ~1.5% above MATLAB <span class="mono">regionprops3</span></b> by construction (§4.8).</li>
<li><b>Meshing is not bit-reproducible across processes</b> (§4.1).</li>
</ul>

</body>
